ARTICLES = [
{"slug":"brazil-betting-ban-provisional-measure-trf3-stake-injunction-reversed",
"title":"Brazil Betting Ban: TRF-3 Reverses Stake Injunction",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"A federal appeals court has undone Stake’s temporary shield from Brazil’s betting ban, leaving licensed operators exposed and the STF undecided.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-24T06:10:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["brazil"],
"related_firms":["pinheiro-neto-advogados","mattos-filho","machado-meyer"],
"body":[
"Brazil’s online betting market is in an unprecedented position. Earlier this month the federal government used a provisional measure to order online betting operators offline, reversing the licensed model that only took effect in 2025. The measure has since been tested in the courts, and the most recent development is a ruling by the Federal Regional Court of the 3rd Region (TRF-3) that vacated a temporary injunction previously granted to Stake by a São Paulo federal judge. For operators, investors and their advisers, the decision is a reminder that individual injunctions are a fragile basis on which to plan a Brazilian business.",
"According to published reports, the São Paulo injunction had allowed Stake to keep operating until 25 October, and its site and app briefly returned on 8 October before going offline again two days later. The Attorney General’s Office (AGU) appealed on an emergency basis, and the TRF-3 judge reportedly reasoned that letting one operator continue to take bets while its competitors were barred created a “regulatory asymmetry” capable of undermining the public policy being implemented. That reasoning matters beyond Stake. It signals that appellate courts may treat piecemeal relief as incompatible with the measure’s objectives, which weakens the case for parallel applications by other operators.",
"The government’s defence of the ban rests on data collected since the regulated framework began, including household indebtedness and problem gambling indicators. That is a proportionality argument, and it is the terrain on which the real constitutional contest will be fought. A provisional measure (medida provisória) is an instrument of urgency that takes immediate effect but must be converted into law by Congress within a fixed period or it lapses. Challengers will therefore press two lines: that the claimed urgency and relevance are not made out for a sector the state had itself just licensed, and that the measure disproportionately interferes with the legitimate expectations of authorised operators.",
"The Supreme Federal Tribunal (STF) is reviewing a constitutional challenge to the measure. Reports indicate that Justice Luiz Fux has signalled he will not rule on suspending it until after the 25 October presidential runoff. Political commentary has also floated the idea of treating sports betting and online casino differently, which could open a path to a partial reopening. Counsel should treat that as a possibility rather than a forecast, but a split regime would have obvious consequences for licence conditions, product scope and the allocation of the existing authorisation fees.",
"The commercial exposure is considerable. One consultancy study cited in the trade press estimates that R$73 billion in tax revenue is at risk over four years and that more than 15,500 jobs could be lost, and industry voices from supplier and sportsbook platforms have publicly warned about sports-integrity and consumer-channelling consequences of pushing demand towards unlicensed sites. Evolution’s chief executive reportedly raised the regulatory uncertainty on the company’s latest earnings call. Licensed operators should assume that unlicensed offshore supply will fill any vacuum, which in turn raises enforcement, payment-blocking and advertising questions for the authorities.",
"Practical steps for operators are fairly clear. First, document compliance with the shutdown order across all channels, including affiliates, to avoid penalties that could compound a later dispute. Second, preserve the evidential record of the authorisation process, capital commitments and fees paid, since any compensation, refund or administrative claim will depend on it. Third, review contracts with suppliers, payment providers and sports-rights holders for force majeure, regulatory-change and termination mechanics. Fourth, coordinate any litigation strategy through trade associations where possible, because the TRF-3 reasoning suggests collective relief is more likely to survive appeal than isolated applications.",
"The editorial view of GamblingLawyers.com is that the next fortnight will determine whether Brazil’s ban becomes a durable policy or a negotiating position. The runoff, the STF’s timetable and Congress’s handling of the provisional measure are the three variables to watch. Operators and investors with Brazilian exposure should speak to specialist counsel in São Paulo and Brasília now, and our directory lists firms active in the market regulatory, tax and constitutional-litigation practices."
]},
{"slug":"curacao-gaming-authority-licensing-portal-breach-licensee-obligations",
"title":"Curaçao Portal Breach: What Licensees Must Do Now",
"category":"Licensing",
"excerpt":"The CGA has scrapped its breached portal and launched a temporary replacement. Licences remain valid, but data-protection duties may be triggered.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-24T07:25:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["curacao","germany","netherlands"],
"related_firms":["gaming-legal-group","kalff-katz-and-franssen","hambach-and-hambach"],
"body":[
"The Curaçao Gaming Authority (CGA) has confirmed a prolonged security incident affecting its licensing portal, and the fallout is relevant to every licence holder and applicant under the new Curaçao regime. According to the regulator, unauthorised access to the portal and its database took place between December 2025 and September 2026. It discovered the breach on 17 September 2026, after which the portal was taken offline for an external investigation, and on 7 October it announced that it would stop using the portal and the provider’s regulatory services permanently.",
"The CGA has said that a temporary portal was scheduled to launch on 19 October to keep applications and supervision moving, with the licence register and the digital seal system returning alongside it. The client-facing portal reportedly still requires adjustments and security testing, and the authority intends to build and operate its own replacement. The regulator has also stated that closing the previous portal does not invalidate existing licences and that licensees must continue to meet their obligations. That statement is important: operators should not treat the disruption as a pause on compliance deadlines.",
"The breach touches information that licensees were required to submit, and the CGA says the individual responsible accessed and published confidential information belonging to online gambling licence holders. The regulator has described the conduct as criminal, reported it to authorities in Curaçao, Germany and the Netherlands, and engaged firms to remove the material online. An independent investigation is assessing the impact, and civil action is being considered. The categories of data involved have not been fully specified publicly, so licensees cannot assume that their own submissions were unaffected.",
"For licensees the first question is data-protection exposure. If personal data of directors, shareholders, key persons or customers was submitted through the portal, the operator may need to assess whether it has notification or documentation duties under the data-protection law that applies to it, including the GDPR where individuals in the EU are concerned. The controller for that purpose is not always obvious, and the analysis should be documented promptly. Operators with EU-facing business or EU-resident officers should take advice in the relevant member state without waiting for the CGA’s investigation to conclude.",
"The second question concerns the licensing timeline. Curaçao’s reform replaced the master-licence sub-licence model with direct licences, and a central deadline for suppliers is approaching in December 2026. Applicants who had lodged materials through the old portal should confirm in writing that the CGA holds a complete file, resubmit through the temporary portal where asked, and retain proof of the original submission date. Where a regulatory deadline falls inside the disruption window, a written request for confirmation or extension is cheaper than a later dispute about timeliness.",
"The third issue is counterparty and banking diligence. Payment providers, acquirers and B2B partners rely on the CGA licence register and seal as evidence of authorisation. Operators should be ready to give counterparties a clear statement that their licence remains valid, supported by CGA correspondence, and should check that the restored seal is correctly implemented. Gaming sites in regulated markets such as Germany and the Netherlands, where the regulators have publicly scrutinised Curaçao-licensed operators, will want accurate licence evidence on hand.",
"The editorial view of GamblingLawyers.com is that the incident is a credibility test for a licensing regime still in transition. The CGA’s decision to build its own portal is sensible, but operators should expect more detailed security and data-handling questions from counterparties in the coming months. Licensees and applicants should speak to counsel experienced in Curaçao and in the data-protection law of their target markets, and our directory can help identify them."
]},
{"slug":"peru-isc-online-betting-tax-bill-five-percent-operator-impact",
"title":"Peru Bill to Raise Online Betting Tax to 5% ISC",
"category":"Tax",
"excerpt":"A Peruvian bill would lift the ISC on remote gambling from 1% to 5% of each bet, on top of the 12% operator tax. What operators should model.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-24T08:40:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["peru","brazil","colombia"],
"related_firms":["pinheiro-neto-advogados","mattos-filho","bird-and-bird"],
"body":[
"A bill submitted in Peru’s Congress would raise the Selective Consumption Tax (Impuesto Selectivo al Consumo, ISC) on remote gambling and sports betting from 1% to 5%. The proposal is by congressman Luis Aurelio Masco Cáceres and, according to trade reports, would apply the higher rate to the value of each taxable bet while leaving the existing tax base, assessment rules, audit provisions and foreign-platform criteria unchanged. It is a proposal only and has not been approved, but it arrives while the regional tax debate is dominated by Brazil’s experience and deserves attention from operators in the Peruvian market.",
"The current structure matters for understanding the proposal. Regulations published in December 2024 extended the ISC to online gambling, with a temporary 0.3% rate until 30 June 2025 and 1% since 1 July 2025. The tax arises when funds or cash-valued bonuses are debited to place a bet. Separately, Law 31557 imposes a tax on operators at 12% of the monthly taxable base after deductions for winnings, refunds and a platform-maintenance allowance. Because the two levies apply to different bases, they cannot simply be added together, and any modelling exercise must treat them separately.",
"That distinction is the crux of the commercial analysis. A 5% tax on the stake rather than on net revenue is a turnover-style charge, and its burden rises sharply as margins fall. For a sportsbook running at a single-digit gross margin, a five-percentage-point charge on handle could consume most or all of the margin before the 12% operator tax and other costs are considered. High-volume, low-margin products such as in-play and parlays would feel it most, and promotional spend that is debited as cash-valued bonus could be taxed as well. Operators should run scenarios at the product level and not on blended figures.",
"Revenue projections cited in the press suggest annual ISC receipts could grow from roughly S/324 million to about S/1.62 billion, an increase of around S/1.3 billion. Those estimates assume stable betting volumes, which is the weakest assumption in the model. Higher turnover taxes in other jurisdictions have produced some migration to unlicensed supply, lower handle and downward pressure on promotions, and the Peruvian authorities will need to weigh those effects against collection targets. Sunat and the Ministry of Economy and Finance would oversee implementation, and the higher rate would start on the first day of the calendar month after the law takes effect.",
"There are legal questions as well. The structure of an ISC on remote gambling, the treatment of foreign platforms and the interaction with the operator tax have all been the subject of commentary about proportionality and constitutional principles of taxation. If the bill advances, expect operators and industry associations to argue that a fivefold increase on a turnover base is confiscatory in effect, and to seek transitional arrangements. Counsel should monitor committee stages for amendments to the base and to the treatment of bonuses.",
"In practical terms operators should do four things now. Model the 5% scenario against actual margin by product. Review the platform and payment-flow documentation that determines the moment a tax liability arises. Examine contracts with affiliates and partners that are priced on revenue share, since a higher tax will change the economics of those agreements. And engage in the legislative process through an industry body, since early technical input on the base and on bonus treatment is often decisive.",
"The editorial view of GamblingLawyers.com is that the bill should be watched rather than assumed. Many tax proposals are amended or shelved before enactment. But the direction of travel in Latin America is towards higher gambling taxes, and operators with Peruvian exposure should have their scenarios ready. Specialist tax counsel in Lima and in regional hubs can advise, and our directory lists practitioners across the region."
]},
{"slug":"acma-formal-warnings-former-afl-players-illegal-gambling-promotion",
"title":"ACMA Warns Ex-AFL Players Over Illegal Gambling Ads",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"ACMA has issued formal warnings to two former AFL players for promoting a prohibited online casino, signalling scrutiny of influencers.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-24T09:15:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["australia","united-kingdom"],
"related_firms":["harris-hagan","bird-and-bird","pinsent-masons-llp"],
"body":[
"The Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA) has issued formal warnings to two former Australian Football League players, Mitch Robinson and Rhys Mathieson, for promoting Vegastars, an online gambling service that is prohibited in Australia. The regulator’s media release is dated 22 October 2026. The warnings are not fines, but they are a public enforcement outcome and they illustrate how Australian authorities now treat individuals who lend their profile to illegal operators.",
"According to ACMA, the promotion took place between March and April 2026 on Instagram and on the YouTube channel of Robinson’s podcast. The content reportedly included the service’s name, logo, website address, gameplay footage and promotional commentary. Both men accepted responsibility, ended their involvement and removed the material. In January 2026 ACMA had asked internet service providers to block the Vegastars website, so the promotional activity followed, rather than preceded, regulatory action against the service itself. A similar warning was reportedly issued to a mixed martial arts fighter earlier in the year.",
"The legal basis is the Interactive Gambling Act, which prohibits the provision of certain online gambling services to Australian customers and contains rules on promoting prohibited services. ACMA has made clear that these warnings rely on the existing prohibitions and do not relate to the notable-persons advertising restrictions that start on 1 January 2027. That distinction is important for compliance advice: the present risk does not depend on the new regime, and anyone promoting an unlicensed service today is exposed under the current law.",
"Statutory exposure is substantial even if the outcome here was a warning. ACMA has cited maximum civil penalties of AU$65,520 per contravention for an individual, and AU$2,730,000 for facilitating access through hyperlinks or directions to a prohibited service. Those are ceilings, not penalties imposed in this case, but they show why warnings should be understood as the first rung on an enforcement ladder. Repeat or knowing conduct, or promotion by commercial entities such as affiliates and agencies, can sit at a much higher level of risk.",
"For athletes, creators and their agents the lessons are practical. Due diligence before any sponsorship or ambassador deal should include checking whether the counterparty holds a licence in the market where the audience is located, and whether the service is on ACMA’s list of blocked sites. Contracts should contain warranties of licensing status, termination rights if the service becomes unlawful, and takedown obligations. Social media content is persistent, so a withdrawn deal does not remove the evidential record, and removal should be prompt and documented.",
"Licensed operators and their marketing partners have a parallel interest. Affiliate programmes that route Australian traffic to unlicensed brands can create reputational and, for groups with multiple brands, regulatory risk elsewhere. Regulators in the UK and Europe watch enforcement abroad, and group compliance functions should align their affiliate and influencer vetting with the strictest jurisdiction in which they operate. The forthcoming Australian restrictions on notable persons will add a further layer for licensed wagering providers.",
"The editorial view of GamblingLawyers.com is that ACMA’s approach combines blocking, naming and warning, and that the next step against persistent promoters is likely to be more severe. Influencers, agencies and operators with Australian-facing audiences should review their arrangements now. Specialist gambling counsel can advise on promotion rules, and our directory lists firms with relevant experience across jurisdictions."
]},
{"slug":"netherlands-ksa-record-fines-unlicensed-operators-novatech-fortaprime",
"title":"Dutch KSA Record Fines: Lessons for Offshore Operators",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"The KSA’s record penalties on unlicensed operators, including €24.8m on Novatech, show no tolerance for Dutch-facing offshore sites.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-24T10:05:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["netherlands","curacao","germany"],
"related_firms":["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers"],
"body":[
"The Netherlands Gambling Authority (Kansspelautoriteit, KSA) has built a consistent enforcement record against unlicensed operators, and 2026 has brought the largest penalties yet. In March the regulator fined Novatech, which operates the Qbet and 55Bet brands, a record €24.8 million, and imposed a €1.8 million fine on Fortaprime SRL, whose brands included Amonbet, Supraplay, Bilucky, Gxspins, Kaasino, Hiddenjack and Luckymax7. Trade press this week has also reported a further record fine, reportedly around €37 million, against an unlicensed operator running more than ten sites; details should be confirmed from the KSA’s own decision before they are relied on.",
"The reported findings in the March decisions are instructive. Both operators lacked a Dutch licence, yet Dutch customers could open accounts and deposit funds. The KSA also found that no technical measures blocked access from the Netherlands, and it identified several aggravating violations. The legal framework is the Remote Gambling Act (Wet Kansspelen op afstand), in force since 2021, which makes it unlawful to offer remote gambling to Dutch players without a licence and gives the KSA power to impose administrative fines and orders subject to periodic penalty payments.",
"Several features of the KSA’s method explain the size of the penalties. The authority calculates fines by reference to the seriousness and duration of the infringement and the operator’s revenue from the Dutch market, and it increases amounts where there are aggravating factors such as deliberate targeting or the absence of any attempt to restrict access. Its published guidelines also allow for a penalty to reflect the need for deterrence. A decision that an operator did nothing to geo-block the Netherlands is therefore not a technicality; it is a factor that pushes the fine upward.",
"Targeting is the central legal concept. The KSA looks at whether a service is directed at the Dutch market, using indicators such as Dutch-language content, the euro currency, Dutch payment methods such as iDEAL, marketing aimed at Dutch consumers, and the ability of Dutch residents to register and deposit. Operators who hold a licence in another jurisdiction, such as Curaçao, cannot rely on that licence as a defence, because the Dutch regime requires authorisation from the KSA itself. Mere disclaimers in the terms and conditions will not be sufficient if the website remains accessible and functional for Dutch players.",
"The consequences extend beyond fines. A violation finding can be a significant obstacle to later licence applications, since the KSA assesses reliability and integrity of applicants and their affiliates and can take past conduct into account. Payment providers, affiliates and suppliers that work with unlicensed Dutch-facing sites are also at risk from the regulator’s focus on the wider ecosystem, including its engagement on advertising and banking. Operators planning market entry should therefore treat past Dutch-facing activity as a matter requiring early legal analysis.",
"Practical steps follow. Operators without Dutch licences should carry out a documented geo-blocking and targeting review covering registration, payments, marketing and affiliate traffic. They should test whether Dutch IP addresses and payment instruments are actually refused, and keep records of the tests. Groups with multiple brands should check that their separate sites do not share infrastructure or marketing that could attribute Dutch targeting to the group. And any company that receives a KSA information request or intention to fine should obtain Dutch counsel immediately, because response deadlines are short and the objection procedure is strict.",
"The editorial view of GamblingLawyers.com is that the trend in penalty size is firmly upward and that the KSA is willing to make examples of operators that disregard the licensing requirement. Companies considering a Dutch licence application, or facing enquiries, should consult specialist counsel in the Netherlands, and our directory lists firms with deep experience before the KSA."
]},
]
